That restriction is not about the code. It is about what the code is pointed
at, and what it puts on a screen.

This application has no data of its own. Every price, sale, and population
count it displays was collected from a website run by real people who never
asked to be scraped. Running a copy of this means pointing it at such an
archive — either someone else's, or one you filled yourself by running a
copy of the scraper, which is the thing that companion project asks you not
to do. The front end inherits that responsibility at one remove; it does not
escape it.

There is a second reason, and it is the one that changes with this repo
rather than the scraper. Storing someone's data is a private act. Serving it
to the public is not. Card images and market figures shown to visitors are
distribution, and the people whose data it is get a say in that which they
never gave.

None of this is a claim that the ideas are precious. The architecture, the
sufficiency states, the decision ledger, the reasoning in docs/adr/ — take
all of it, and ask me anything about any of it. Just don't stand up another
copy pointed at their data.

Fan-made. Not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to Nintendo, The
Pokémon Company, Creatures Inc., GAME FREAK, or PriceCharting.
